      "index": 0,
      "type": "text",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "score": 39,
      "reasoning": "This is an Objective (Mode A) criterion. The report provides extensive quantitative results and obtains a cumulative loss and period rates reasonably near the published values, but its reconstruction underestimates 2012–2023 loss and the 2023 maximum, does not report the required 36 ± 10% acceleration, and entirely omits the quantitative comparison showing glaciers exceed Greenland and Antarctic ice-sheet contributions; its non-replicative methodology also uses 257 rather than the specified 233 estimates."
     },
     {
      "index": 1,
      "type": "image",
      "weight": 0.1,
      "score": 29,
      "reasoning": "Mode A. The report quantitatively identifies the major absolute contributors with roughly comparable shares when Arctic Canada north and south are combined (Alaska 24%, Canadian Arctic 18%, Greenland periphery 11%, Southern Andes 12%), but these deviate from the target values and ranking. Crucially, it does not calculate or visualize cumulative loss relative to 2000 ice volume, so the specified −39% to −20% high-relative-loss results are absent; specific annual rates are not an equivalent metric."
     },
     {
      "index": 2,
      "type": "image",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "score": 18,
      "reasoning": "This is Objective (Mode A). The report includes a method-agreement plot, but it combines DEM and glaciological observations, covers only 13 regions, and does not reproduce the target’s separate DEM baseline or the key mean differences (altimetry 0.08 ± 0.08, gravimetry 0.02 ± 0.08, glaciological bias −0.10 ± 0.10 m w.e. yr⁻¹); therefore the central quantitative comparison cannot be verified."
     },
     {
      "index": 3,
      "type": "image",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "score": 0,
      "reasoning": "Mode A (objective quantitative/visual comparison). None of the AI figures compares observations with low/high-emission projections through 2040–2100; the closest cumulative plot ends in 2024 and only compares two observational reconstructions, so the target’s scenario ranges, exceedance of low-end projections, and future outlook are absent."
     }
